WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.360 --> 00:00:02.310
<v Speaker>So let's go and practice using CloudFront,</v>

2
00:00:02.310 --> 00:00:04.860
but first we have to create an S3 bucket

3
00:00:04.860 --> 00:00:07.560
to hold our files for our distribution.

4
00:00:07.560 --> 00:00:08.580
So let's create a bucket,

5
00:00:08.580 --> 00:00:12.913
and I'll call this one demo-CloudFront-Stephan-v4,

6
00:00:14.370 --> 00:00:17.370
and it will scroll down, leave everything as the default

7
00:00:17.370 --> 00:00:19.143
and click on Create Bucket.

8
00:00:20.460 --> 00:00:22.230
Okay, so my bucket is now created

9
00:00:22.230 --> 00:00:25.290
and I'm going to upload some files in it.

10
00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:26.490
So let me add files

11
00:00:26.490 --> 00:00:28.110
and I will choose my three files,

12
00:00:28.110 --> 00:00:31.830
beach, coffee and index.html.

13
00:00:31.830 --> 00:00:33.003
So let's upload them.

14
00:00:34.230 --> 00:00:35.430
And now that they've been uploaded,

15
00:00:35.430 --> 00:00:37.890
so if I go in my bucket, we can see all of them.

16
00:00:37.890 --> 00:00:39.960
And as a reminder, if I go on index.html,

17
00:00:39.960 --> 00:00:41.700
I have two ways of open it.

18
00:00:41.700 --> 00:00:44.100
The first one is to use the object URL.

19
00:00:44.100 --> 00:00:44.933
But if I try it,

20
00:00:44.933 --> 00:00:48.750
I get an access denied because my object is not public.

21
00:00:48.750 --> 00:00:51.210
And if I go and click on open here,

22
00:00:51.210 --> 00:00:53.250
this generates a pre-sign.

23
00:00:53.250 --> 00:00:54.600
So let's allow popups.

24
00:00:54.600 --> 00:00:58.530
This generates a pre-signed URL for my S3 objects,

25
00:00:58.530 --> 00:01:00.030
which allows me to access the object.

26
00:01:00.030 --> 00:01:01.470
So you see "I love coffee" and "hello world"

27
00:01:01.470 --> 00:01:03.000
but I still don't see the image

28
00:01:03.000 --> 00:01:05.460
because the image itself is not public.

29
00:01:05.460 --> 00:01:08.400
So let's see how we can instead use CloudFront

30
00:01:08.400 --> 00:01:12.003
to make these files accessible without making them public.

31
00:01:12.900 --> 00:01:15.753
So let's open up the CloudFront console.

32
00:01:16.860 --> 00:01:18.840
And I first have this popup about pricing,

33
00:01:18.840 --> 00:01:20.670
so I'm going to click on don't show it again

34
00:01:20.670 --> 00:01:22.800
and then close it.

35
00:01:22.800 --> 00:01:26.310
So let's go ahead and create a CloudFront distribution.

36
00:01:26.310 --> 00:01:29.190
So first we have to do is to select a plan.

37
00:01:29.190 --> 00:01:31.320
So now there are several plans for CloudFront,

38
00:01:31.320 --> 00:01:34.710
and as you can see, they have different features.

39
00:01:34.710 --> 00:01:35.940
But if we have a look,

40
00:01:35.940 --> 00:01:37.770
the free plan is going to be enough for us.

41
00:01:37.770 --> 00:01:41.070
So we have enough requests and allowance per month,

42
00:01:41.070 --> 00:01:43.290
we're going to get always on DNS protection,

43
00:01:43.290 --> 00:01:46.020
we're going to get geographic traffic blocking

44
00:01:46.020 --> 00:01:48.690
and global CDN, DNS, free TL certificates.

45
00:01:48.690 --> 00:01:51.000
So all of this is enough for us

46
00:01:51.000 --> 00:01:52.560
for what we need for CloudFront.

47
00:01:52.560 --> 00:01:55.230
But if you needed something such as, for example,

48
00:01:55.230 --> 00:01:58.140
edge key-value store or advanced DDoS protection

49
00:01:58.140 --> 00:01:59.550
or uptime SLA,

50
00:01:59.550 --> 00:02:02.580
or protection for WordPress, these kind of things,

51
00:02:02.580 --> 00:02:05.610
then one of these plans will be better for you.

52
00:02:05.610 --> 00:02:07.710
And finally, you have the pay as you go option,

53
00:02:07.710 --> 00:02:10.890
which is to pay for based on the traffic you use.

54
00:02:10.890 --> 00:02:12.210
And then you're going to have

55
00:02:12.210 --> 00:02:14.190
to pay extra for some features.

56
00:02:14.190 --> 00:02:16.470
But to keep things simple, let's keep as a free

57
00:02:16.470 --> 00:02:19.050
because this is going to be enough for our use case.

58
00:02:19.050 --> 00:02:23.840
So this one is going to be called demo new CloudFront.

59
00:02:25.830 --> 00:02:27.660
And here we have only the option

60
00:02:27.660 --> 00:02:29.790
to use a single site or app.

61
00:02:29.790 --> 00:02:32.460
We don't need to have a domain, but we could add a domain

62
00:02:32.460 --> 00:02:34.290
and provision a TLS certificate for us.

63
00:02:34.290 --> 00:02:35.550
Let's click on next.

64
00:02:35.550 --> 00:02:38.640
And now we have to say, what is the origin type?

65
00:02:38.640 --> 00:02:40.530
So as you can see right now, we have Amazon S3,

66
00:02:40.530 --> 00:02:42.930
elastic load balancer, API gateway,

67
00:02:42.930 --> 00:02:45.450
elemental media package or other.

68
00:02:45.450 --> 00:02:47.670
And the VPC origin,

69
00:02:47.670 --> 00:02:50.820
if you want to connect directly to a private EC2 instance

70
00:02:50.820 --> 00:02:54.210
or an application of balancer that are deployed privately,

71
00:02:54.210 --> 00:02:56.070
this is only available in the business plan.

72
00:02:56.070 --> 00:02:59.160
But still an option of what CloudFront can do.

73
00:02:59.160 --> 00:03:00.930
So we'll use Amazon S3.

74
00:03:00.930 --> 00:03:03.780
And then we have to browse Amazon S3.

75
00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:07.200
And we'll use a demo CloudFront stephane v4.

76
00:03:07.200 --> 00:03:08.346
Okay.

77
00:03:08.346 --> 00:03:11.390
Next we're going to allow private S3 bucket access

78
00:03:11.390 --> 00:03:12.223
to CloudFront.

79
00:03:12.223 --> 00:03:15.060
Yes, and we'll use the recommended origin settings

80
00:03:15.060 --> 00:03:16.830
and we'll use the recommended cache settings

81
00:03:16.830 --> 00:03:18.060
to serve S3 content.

82
00:03:18.060 --> 00:03:20.280
So now the setup is quite simple.

83
00:03:20.280 --> 00:03:21.450
If we want to have security,

84
00:03:21.450 --> 00:03:24.030
we can enable the web application firewall,

85
00:03:24.030 --> 00:03:26.659
but this is not something we need right now.

86
00:03:26.659 --> 00:03:30.270
And if you want to have protection against layer 7, again,

87
00:03:30.270 --> 00:03:32.520
this is available with the business plan.

88
00:03:32.520 --> 00:03:34.890
So right now, I don't want anything.

89
00:03:34.890 --> 00:03:35.853
I'll click on-

90
00:03:37.410 --> 00:03:38.610
Make sure we have the free plan.

91
00:03:38.610 --> 00:03:39.780
Yes, that's good.

92
00:03:39.780 --> 00:03:43.140
The UI is just a little bit counterintuitive in this case,

93
00:03:43.140 --> 00:03:44.280
but let's get back into this.

94
00:03:44.280 --> 00:03:48.150
So web application firewall, we don't need anything.

95
00:03:48.150 --> 00:03:50.370
We click on next and we can review everything.

96
00:03:50.370 --> 00:03:52.680
So yes, we are on the free plan

97
00:03:52.680 --> 00:03:56.403
and we're going to create our distribution.

98
00:04:00.840 --> 00:04:03.570
Okay, so my distribution is now created.

99
00:04:03.570 --> 00:04:07.230
Now if I go into S3 and look at the permissions

100
00:04:07.230 --> 00:04:09.240
and I look at my bucket policy,

101
00:04:09.240 --> 00:04:11.880
I can see that now in my bucket policy,

102
00:04:11.880 --> 00:04:14.370
I have a bucket policy allowing access

103
00:04:14.370 --> 00:04:17.490
to my bucket from our CloudFront distribution.

104
00:04:17.490 --> 00:04:19.410
These are two because I had two,

105
00:04:19.410 --> 00:04:21.720
one is a test and one is the one I just created for you.

106
00:04:21.720 --> 00:04:24.720
As we can see, this allows our CloudFront distribution

107
00:04:24.720 --> 00:04:26.670
to privately access our S3 buckets.

108
00:04:26.670 --> 00:04:29.430
And so this was entered as a bucket policy

109
00:04:29.430 --> 00:04:32.433
by the platform service as it was being deployed.

110
00:04:33.720 --> 00:04:36.120
So my distribution is now ready

111
00:04:36.120 --> 00:04:38.070
and I can click on the domain name,

112
00:04:38.070 --> 00:04:39.870
open a new tab, and press enter.

113
00:04:39.870 --> 00:04:42.120
Now I'm gonna get an access denied, but this is fine.

114
00:04:42.120 --> 00:04:45.030
It's because I need to enter one specific path.

115
00:04:45.030 --> 00:04:47.070
So /coffee.jpg.

116
00:04:47.070 --> 00:04:49.710
As you can see, my coffee image is now loading.

117
00:04:49.710 --> 00:04:53.250
Or /beach.jpeg.

118
00:04:53.250 --> 00:04:54.780
And again, my beach image is loading.

119
00:04:54.780 --> 00:04:58.770
And now if I go to index.html,

120
00:04:58.770 --> 00:04:59.940
I have my full image

121
00:04:59.940 --> 00:05:01.800
with "I love coffee" and "hello world" and this image.

122
00:05:01.800 --> 00:05:06.510
So even though all my bucket objects are private,

123
00:05:06.510 --> 00:05:09.660
now because we have this bucket policy

124
00:05:09.660 --> 00:05:11.370
allowing access from CloudFront,

125
00:05:11.370 --> 00:05:14.850
I'm able through CloudFront, to see all that I need to see.

126
00:05:14.850 --> 00:05:17.430
But now the added benefit is that if I go back

127
00:05:17.430 --> 00:05:20.250
to beach.jpeg, the image is cached

128
00:05:20.250 --> 00:05:23.400
so loading is almost instantaneous.

129
00:05:23.400 --> 00:05:24.510
So back to here.

130
00:05:24.510 --> 00:05:26.250
Again, very, very fast loading.

131
00:05:26.250 --> 00:05:28.350
And that is the benefit of CloudFront.

132
00:05:28.350 --> 00:05:29.183
So that's it.

133
00:05:29.183 --> 00:05:30.810
We've created a CloudFront distribution within

134
00:05:30.810 --> 00:05:33.540
the free plan, and we set up S3 as an origin

135
00:05:33.540 --> 00:05:35.430
and we saw the bucket policy.

136
00:05:35.430 --> 00:05:36.420
So I hope you liked it,

137
00:05:36.420 --> 00:05:38.523
and I will see you in the next lecture.

